# Environment-Stable CoxNiy Encapsulation in Stacked Porous Carbon Nanosheets for Enhanced Microwave Absorption

## abstract
Magnetic/dielectric@porous carbon composites, derived from metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with adjustable composition ratio, have attracted wide attention due to their unique magnetoelectric properties. In addition, MOFs-derived porous carbon-based materials can meet the needs of lightweight feature. This paper reports a simple process for synthesizing stacked CoxNiy@C nanosheets derived from CoxNiy-MOFs nanosheets with multiple interfaces, which is good to the microwave response. The CoxNiy@C with controllable composition can be obtained by adjusting the ratio of Co2+ and Ni2+. It is supposed that the increased Co content is benefit to the dielectric and magnetic loss. Additionally, the bandwidth of CoNi@C nanosheets can take up almost the whole Ku band. Moreover, this composite has better environmental stability in air, which characteristic provides a sustainable potential for the practical application.

Environment‑Stable $\mathbf{Co}_{x}\mathbf{Ni}_{y}$ Encapsulation in Stacked Porous Carbon Nanosheets for Enhanced Microwave Absorption
Cite as Nano-Micro Lett. (2020) 12:102
## HIGHLIGHTS
•	 The microwave absorbing performance of alloy $@\mathrm{C}$ composites can be controlled through regulating ratio of metal ions.
•	 Carbon-based alloy $\boldsymbol{@}\mathrm{C}$ composites exhibit the potential stability of microwave absorption with almost the whole Ku band for the practical application.
ABSTRACT  Magnetic/dielectric $@$ porous carbon composites, derived from metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with adjustable composition ratio, have attracted wide attention due to their unique magnetoelectric properties. In addition, MOFs-derived porous carbon-based materials can meet the needs of lightweight feature. This paper reports a simple process for synthesizing stacked $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets derived from $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ -MOFs nanosheets with multiple interfaces, which is good to the microwave response. The $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ with controllable composition can be obtained by adjusting the ratio of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ . It is supposed that the increased Co content is benefit to the dielectric and magnetic loss. Additionally, the bandwidth of ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ nanosheets can take up almost the whole $\mathtt{K u}$ band. Moreover, this composite has better environmental stability in air, which characteristic provides a sustainable potential for the practical application.
KEYWORDS  MOFs; $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets; Multiple interfaces; Microwave absorption; Environmental stability
## 1  Introduction
With the rapid expansion of communication technology and increasing electromagnetic radiation, it is necessary to achieve multifunctional absorbers [1–3]. Various strict performance requirements such as thin thickness, light weight, wide frequency band, and strong absorption strength have been proposed [4, 5]. Hence, the study of the composition and structure design of the material has been stimulated [6–8]. Among them, magnetic/dielectric [9–11] composites have received more attention due to the excellent dielectric and magnetic losses. For example, Zhou et al. [12] reported a non-uniform $\mathrm{FeCo/ZnO}$ nanosheet that was adjusted by an auxiliary template method to reduce the density and impedance of the composite. By adjusting $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ artificially designed $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ structure, a strong electromagnetic wave response was obtained by Quan et al. [13]. Che et al. [14] reported $\mathbf{CoNi@Air@TiO_{2}}$ yolk-shell structure with outstanding microwave absorption property $\mathrm{(RL=-58.2dB}$ ). Feng et al. [15] also investigated the CoNi alloy combined with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and graphene, and the matching thickness is only $2.0\mathrm{mm}$ . All of them demonstrated the strong magnetic loss caused by CoNi cores. Therefore, the CoNi alloy could be a candidate for the magnetic loss material.
In addition, the ideal absorber should have the characteristics as follows: strong magnetic lossand sufficient dielectric loss [16, 17]. Porous carbon is considered to be a material with high dielectric loss [18, 19]. Moreover, due to the lightweight property of porous carbon, assembling alloy in carbon materials is a commendable choice. However, the problem is that the process of preparing alloy $@$ porous carbon materials by the conventional template method is complicated [20, 21]. Therefore, with a sample method to prepare alloy nanoparticles embedded in porous carbon is a challenge. Metal/oxide nanoporous carbon composites derived from MOFs have an easy-to-access surface area, diverse structural topologies, and adjustable functions, which is a mature synthesis method developed in recent years [22, 23].
In this study, stacked CoNi-MOFs used as a template deriving $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@{\mathrm{C}}$ nanosheets have been investigated. It is worth noting that the carbonization process is important for the formation of porous carbon and $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloys and the stacked structures promote the formation of multiple interfaces. The synthesized $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ composite has a highly developed porous structure. In the derived porous structure, the carbon layer can protect the metal molecules from oxidation [24]. Moreover, the carbon layer can provide a channel for electron transport, which is good for dielectric loss [25, 26]. In addition, for the $\mathrm{CoNi@C(Co^{2+};N i^{2+}=1;1)}$ nanosheets, the maximum reflection loss value is $-43.7\mathrm{dB}$ , and the lower thickness is $1.7\mathrm{mm}$ with the filler loading ratio of $20\mathrm{wt}\%$ . In addition, the effective bandwidth is reaching $5.7\mathrm{GHz}$ with thinner thickness of $1.8\mathrm{mm}$ . This study has shown that $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets are excellent adsorbents because of their light weight, thin thickness, and strong absorption capacity. At the same time, this research also opened up a new way for simply designing multiple interfaces and stable porous nanostructured alloy $@$ carbon nanosheets with targeted functions.
## 2  Experimental Section
## 2.1  Synthesis of $\mathbf{Co}_{x}\mathbf{Ni}_{y}@\mathbf{C}$ Nanocomposites
## 2.2  Structure Characterization
FESEM (field-emission scanning electron microscopy, JEOL, JSM-7100F) and TEM (transmission electron microscopy, JEOL, JEM-2100F) were used to analyze the morphology and microstructure of the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets. Raman spectra (Renishaw INVIA micro-Raman spectroscopy system) and XRD (D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer, $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\alpha$ radiation, $\lambda{=}1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) was used to characterize the structure of the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, VGMultiLab 2000) was used to test the chemical states of elements. Adsorption of nitrogen was used to measure Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area using Tristar II 3020 instrument. Agilent PNA N5244A vector network analyzer (VNA) was used to test the electromagnetic parameters in the range of $2{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ with coaxial wire analysis model [27]. Compressing sample and paraffin with $20\%$ filler loading ratio made a ring with inner and outer diameter of $3.04\mathrm{mm}$ and $7.00\mathrm{mm}$ to measure.
## 3  Results and Discussion
3.1  Structure of $\mathbf{Co}_{\pmb{x}}\mathbf{Ni}_{\pmb{y}}$ ‑MOFs and $\mathbf{Co}_{x}\mathbf{Ni}_{y}@\mathbf{C}$ Composites
In order to comprehend the formation process of $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ clearly, typical synthesis route is shown in Fig. 1a, b. Stacked precursors with nanosheets morphology were synthesized firstly by hydrothermal method. Then, the precursors were placed in a railboat annealing in the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ obtained the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets. In fact, the carbonization process is important for the formation of the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloy and the formation of a porous carbon skeleton, and the stacked nanosheets formed multiple interfaces attenuating microwave. During the calcination process, a partially graphitized carbon layer covered the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ . At the same time, carbon reduces the metal ions $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Ni}^{0}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{0}$ and then melts them into a $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloy according to the feed ratio. Finally, the derived $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloy nanoparticles were implanted in carbon layer to obtain $\mathrm{Co_{\itx}N i_{\mathrm{y}}/C}$ nanosheets.
Figure  1c, d shows the SEM pictures of CoNi-MOF precursor and $\mathrm{CoNi@C}$ nanosheets. Figure  1c exhibits relatively smooth stacked nanosheets morphology with a breadth about $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and Fig. S1a shows the porous cross profile of the CoNi-MOF precursor. After calcination, the stacked structure became loose and the primary smooth surface became rough (Fig. 1d), emerging more porous structure. In addition, each contact layer forms an interface. Figure S1b, c shows the morphology of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Ni}_{7}@\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}@{\mathrm C}$ composites, the morphology of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Ni}_{7}\mathrm{-MOF}$ and ${\bf C o}_{7}{\bf N i}_{3}–{\bf M O F}$ is inserted, respectively. All of the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ exhibited loose porous structure, which is good to microwave absorption. Figure  1e, f show TEM and HRTEM images of $\mathrm{CoNi@C}$ composite, respectively. It can be clearly seen that the nanosheets are stacked. In addition, the $0.206\mathrm{nm}$ lattice fringes (obtained from red area in Fig. 1f) can be observed clearly, which is corresponded to (111) plane spacing of the face-centered cubic of CoNi crystal [13]. At the edge of CoNi alloy, there is the lattice fringe of the amorphous carbon indicating the presence of carbon layer at the outside of the CoNi alloy. Figure 1e presents the $\mathrm{CoNi@C}$ alloy nanoparticles are equably dispersed on the carbon nanosheets, and the selected area electron diffraction insert in Fig. 1e demonstrated the polycrystalline property of the $\mathrm{CoNi@C}$ composites. The energy-dispersive X-ray elemental mappings of $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ are displayed in Fig. S1di, showing the distribution of Co and Ni elements. Figures S1d, e show the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Ni}_{7}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets elements mapping, which indicated the content of $C0$ is lower than Ni. Figures S1f, $\mathbf{g}$ show the ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ nanosheets elements mapping, which illustrated the content of $\mathrm{Co}$ is nearly to
Ni. Figures S1h, i show the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Ni}_{7}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets elements mapping, which stated the content of $\mathrm{Co}$ is more than Ni. All of results are corresponding to the synthesis progress. In addition, according to the TG analysis in Fig. S2a, the carbon contents in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Ni}_{7}@\mathrm{C}$ , ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ , and $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}@{\mathrm C}$ are evaluated to be $8.83\%$ , $9.82\%$ , and $10.28\%$ , respectively. It could be concluded that the weight loss from 0 to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is water, and the weight loss from 100 to $1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is carbon in the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ composite [28].
The successful synthesis could be proven by XRD pattern of the obtained $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ composites (Fig. 2a). The (111), (200), and (220) faces peaks of face-centered cubic $(f c c)\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloy are matched between $40^{\circ}$ and $80^{\circ}$ [13], in which the fcc atomic structure diagrams can be seen from Fig. 2b. Additionally, all peak locations are resembled to fcc Ni (JCDPS No. 15-0806) or fcc Co (JCDPS No. 01-1260) [13]. Furthermore, no other impurity peak was found, indicating that only the pure $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloys were synthesized. In addition, it can be known from the Raman in Fig. 2c, there are carbon layers in the composites. In general, the D-band is relative to the local defects and disorders carbon [29]. The G-band at $1587~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is supported to the $E_{2\mathrm{g}}$ phonon of $s p^{2}$ bonds of carbon atoms, corresponded to the carbon graphitization degree [30]. As shown in Fig. 2c, with the increase in $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co}$ content, the G-band shows higher values, especially for the $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}@{\mathrm C}$ composites, which indicated existing much graphitic carbon nanostructure in the $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}@{\mathrm C}$ nanosheets. It is because the Co metal could catalyze the formation of graphitic carbon [31]. Additionally, in order to illustrate the influence of heating treatment temperature on ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ composites, the Raman spectra of ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ is shown in Fig. S2b. It also demonstrated that the $I_{\mathrm{G}}/I_{\mathrm{D}}$ values were increased with the high temperature. It can be inferred that the calcination temperature is conducive to the degree of graphitization of carbon, which will tune the electromagnetic wave loss capability [32]. However, the much graphitic carbon may result in higher conductivity, which prejudice against microwave absorption. In addition, the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets also possess the nanoporous structures. In Fig. 2d, the specific surface areas of all the composites were tested. The BET surface areas of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Ni}_{7}@\mathrm{C}$ , ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ , and $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}@{\mathrm C}$ composites are 167.32, 178.69, and $223.74~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which become larger as the $\mathrm{Co}$ content increases. From the pores size distribution shown in Fig. S2c, it can be seen the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets appear nanoporous structure, which could provide more contact site for microwave attenuation. The enhanced specific surface areas illustrate the presence of more pore structures with the increasing Co content. Moreover, XPS was used to further analyze the chemical valences and elemental composition. XPS survey spectrum of the ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ nanosheets is presented in Fig. S3a. Obvious peaks of C, Co, and Ni elements were obtained. In addition, the intensity of $\mathrm{Co}$ and Ni peaks increases with the increase in ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ concentration. It is obviously noted that the $\mathrm{Co}2p$ (Fig. 2e) and $\mathrm{Ni}2p$ (Fig. 2f) were all observed in the three samples; moreover, the $\mathrm{Co}2p$ and Ni $2p$ peaks of ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ in Fig. S3b, c demonstrated the presence of Co and Ni metal [33], which also indicated the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloy. The $\mathrm{~C~}1s$ in Fig. S3d could also illustrate the formation of $\mathrm{^c}$ .
## 3.2  Microwave absorption
In order to explore the electromagnetic wave absorption performance of the composites, reflection loss (RL) values of
all samples with $20~\mathrm{wt}\%$ filler loading ratios are displayed in Fig. 3a–c. Based on Eqs. 1 and 2 [34, 35]:
$$
{\mathrm{RL}}=20\log\left|{\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_{0}}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}+Z_{0}}}\right|
$$
$$
Z_{\mathrm{in}}=Z_{0}{\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}}\operatorname{tanh}\left(j{\frac{2\pi f d}{c}}{\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}}\right)
$$
where $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ and $Z_{0}$ are the input impedance of the absorber and impedance of air. $\varepsilon_{r}$ and $\textstyle\mu_{r}$ are normalized complex permittivity and permeability of the absorber. $f,d,$ , and $\boldsymbol{c}$ represent the frequency of incident microwaves, the thickness of absorber, and the velocity of light, respectively. In general, the RL value below $-10~\mathrm{dB}$ indicates that $90\%$ of the microwave is absorbed and it can be considered as an effective absorption. However, in practical applications, there is a strong requirement for wide bandwidth and thin matching thickness. From reflection loss contour map at different thickness in Fig. 3a–c, it can be seen compared with $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Ni}_{7}@\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}@{\mathrm C}$ composites, and ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ nanosheets could be obtained evident better microwave absorption performance with below $2~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness. In addition, the 3D reflection loss map could more visually present the RL values of the composites. Compared with RL values of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{Ni}_{7}@\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S4a) and $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}@{\mathrm C}$ (Fig. S4b) composites, the $\mathrm{CoNi@C}$ sample (Fig. 3d) exhibited the strongest microwave absorption performance with − 43.7 dB at $1.7~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness (Fig. 3e). Furthermore, the effective absorption bandwidth $5.7\:\mathrm{GHz}$ of ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ nanosheets in almost whole $\mathtt{K u}$ band could be obtained only with thicknesses of $1.8\mathrm{mm}$ (Fig. 3f).
In order to verify the fact that the $\mathrm{CoNi@C}$ composites obtained at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ possess the best microwave absorption performance, the electromagnetic characteristics of ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ composites with different calcination temperatures are compared in Fig. S5. It can be seen that as the calcination temperature increases, the dielectric constant gradually increases (Fig. S5a, b), and the variety in magnetic permeability (Fig. S5c) is not obvious. The increase in the dielectric constant indicates that the dielectric attenuation characteristics of the composite are enhanced, which is conducive to electromagnetic wave absorption. However, through the comparison of RL, it was found that the reflectance of S-700 (Fig. S5d) and S-900 (Fig. S5e) samples did not reach $-10~\mathrm{dB}$ , and S-800 (Fig. 3e) showed excellent reflection loss.
To explore the maximum absorption bandwidth below $2~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness, the effective frequency bandwidth of ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ composites with $1.5{-}2~\mathrm{mm}$ is shown in Fig. 4a. By comparison, the broadest absorption bandwidth could only be acquired at $1.8~\mathrm{mm}$ thickness. Another method to evaluate the microwave absorption property is performed in Fig. 4b, c and Table S1. $\mathrm{{SRL}_{\mathrm{{mf}}}}$ (RL/matching thickness) (Fig. 4b) and $\mathbf{SRL}_{\mathrm{fmt}}$ (RL/(filler loading $\times$ matching thickness)) of ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ nanosheets were calculated with comparing these values with reported carbon-based nanosheets materials. Obviously, the much higher $S\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{mt}}$ and $\mathbf{SRL}_{\mathrm{fmt}}$ values of $\mathrm{CoNi@C}$ composites outclass the reported composites, which implied the better prospect for ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ as an ultrathin, ultralight, and highly effective microwave absorber. In order to clear the cause of microwave absorption gap with three different samples, the electromagnetic parameters are analyzed in Fig. 4d-f. The values of $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ (Fig. 4d) and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ (Fig. 4e) decreased in the $2{\mathrm{-}}18\ \mathrm{GHz}$ range, which exhibited frequency dispersion effect benefited to incident microwave dissipation, conductivity, and dielectric loss. The $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ are increased with the added content of Co, which also illustrate the catalytic effect on graphitized carbon [30]. Although the decline of the $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ is not good to the dielectric loss, the tangent $(\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\varepsilon}{=}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime})$ [36] (Fig. 4h) illustrate that the dielectric loss was increased with the addition of Co. At the same time, analogical trends also emerged in complex permeability $\mathbf{\bar{\boldsymbol{\mu}}}^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ in Fig. 4f), which indicated outstanding magnetic loss behavior. The magnetic losses are usually associated with natural resonance, exchange resonance, and eddy current loss [37].The eddy current loss is determined by $C_{0}\left(C_{0}{=}\mu^{\prime}(\mu^{\prime\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}{=}2{\cal{I}}{\mu}_{0}d^{2}\sigma/3\right)$ [38], if the main reason for magnetic loss is the eddy current loss, the $C_{0}$ values are constant. It is obvious that the $C_{0}$ value fluctuates and decreases in $2{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ frequency range (Fig. S6b).
Therefore, eddy current loss is not the dominant mechanism of magnetic loss, so the exchange resonance and natural resonance should be noticed. The natural resonance usually takes place from 0.1 to $10\:\mathrm{GHz}$ [39]. Hence, the peaks of $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ at $6\:\mathrm{GHz}$ (Fig. 4f) are related to the natural resonance. The two peaks of $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ at 11.5 and $15\:\mathrm{GHz}$ (Fig. 4f) are relevant to exchange resonance. In addition, the natural resonance and exchange resonance are all enhanced due to the improved magnetism by Co, in spite of the only the enhancement of $\mathrm{Co}_{7}\mathrm{Ni}_{3}@{\mathrm C}$ complex is more obvious. Although the natural and the exchange resonance processes cause a decrease of $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ , the magnetic loss still was enhanced with the increase in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ content in $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ composites.
The M-H loop [40] of $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets variation up with increased content of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ is shown in Fig. $4\mathrm{g}$ to further prove the increased magnetic property. The saturation magnetization value (Fig. 4g) was much lower than pure $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloy, which is because the dielectric carbon layer wrapped outside of the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloy. It is general to know the magnetic tangent $(\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\mu}{=}\mu^{\prime\prime}/\mu^{\prime})$ [36] are dominant criterion for evaluating magnetic loss. With the increased content of Co, themagnetic loss (Fig. 4i) was increased. Therefore, the increase of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ content improved dielectric and magnetic loss at the same time, which also enhanced the impedance matching $(Z_{r}=Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0})$ . $Z_{r}$ value close to 1 indicates better impedance matching. From Fig. S6a, one can find that the ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ composites achieved the best impedance matching. Therefore, it illustrates that appropriate ratio of $C_{0}$ and Ni content was good for dielectric loss, while a superfluous was against the impedance matching, resulting in the miserable microwave absorbing performance.
Furthermore, in order to illustrate the microwave absorption stability of the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ composites, the $\mathrm{CoNi@C}$ samples as representative were exposed in air for 1 year later to test the microwave absorption.All the measured values of $\varepsilon^{\prime},\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ , and $\mu^{\prime}$ of ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ and $\mathrm{CoNi@C-1}$ (Fig. 5a, b) shown declined a little bit after exposing in air for 1 year later. Additionally, the RL values (Fig. 5c) and effective bandwidth (Fig.  5d) of $\mathbf{CoNi@C-1}$ composites further proved the stability. Although the decline of the permittivity and permeability unavoidably weakens the attenuation ability for microwave, the $\mathrm{CoNi@C-1}$ composites still appeared better RL loss of − 35 dB with $1.85\mathrm{mm}$ thickness (Fig. 5c). Broadband effective absorption bandwidths could be successfully reached $5.1\mathrm{GHz}$ with thickness of $2.15\mathrm{mm}$ . Therefore, the ${\mathrm{CoNi@C}}$ composites can keep better stability in air for 1 year or longer time with strong microwave response.
In addition to the mechanism of microwave attenuation described above, the conduction loss is another important factor to consume electromagnetic energy. Figure 6a presents atomic structure diagrams of the fcc Co and fcc Ni forming fcc CoNi alloy, which structure increased the stability of the CoNi alloy particles. Moreover, the Co amount affecting the dielectric properties is also proved by density functional theory (DFT) calculations [41]. Because of the increased Co content, the strong conductive loss was good to microwave attenuation.The mechanism of electromagnetic energy conversion in this study can be well revealed, based on the original work reported by Cao and his co-workers that electron transport and dipole polarization do competitive synergy on electromagnetic attenuation [7]. In Fig. 6b, the mechanism of microwave absorption is presented comprehensively, including electron transmission conduction loss, stacked porous nanosheets providing more contact site for microwave, dipole polarization between the CoNi alloy and carbon layer, and dielectric and magnetic loss. Among them, electron transmission conduction loss mainly come from the carbon nanosheets, and the modes of electron transmission could be explained by Yuan et al. [42, 43]. Both electron transport and dipole polarization have great impact on high-performance electromagnetic attenuation, which can be well explained by their competitive synergy originally reported by Cao et al. [44, 45]. Additionally, the stacked nanosheets could also form interlayer interfaces and the $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles could provide multiple interfaces, when electromagnetic waves enter different interfaces, the attenuation degree of loss is different; therefore, multiple interfaces allow electromagnetic waves to be attenuated to a greater extent.
## 4  Conclusion
In summary, the stacked $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets were successfully synthesized by adding CoNi-MOF derived changing with ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ . The microwave absorption loss mechanism included interfaces attenuation brought by stacked structure, conduction loss induced by electron transport, dielectric loss created by carbon, magnetic loss, natural and exchange resonance caused by $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ alloy, and dipole polarization brought by defective carbon and $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles. Microwave absorption performance with a minimum RL value of $-43.7\mathrm{dB}$ with $1.7\mathrm{mm}$ thin thickness and an effective absorption bandwidth of $5.7\mathrm{GHz}$ with $1.8\mathrm{mm}$ thickness could be achieved with a lower filler loading ratio of $20\mathrm{wt}\%$ . Benefiting from the abrasive porous nanosheets structure, it can provide more exposure site for microwave scattering. Therefore, stacked CoNi-MOF-derived multiple interfaces $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}@\mathrm{C}$ nanosheets provided new ideas for the synthesis of alloy $\boldsymbol{@}\mathbf{C}$ composites and increase applications in the microwave absorption field.
